WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.002
Axum applications often rely on Tower
layers and middleware for cross-cutting

2
00:00:07.082 --> 00:00:07.955
behavior.

3
00:00:08.035 --> 00:00:13.858
Tracing, compression, authentication,
CORS, timeouts, body limits,

4
00:00:13.938 --> 00:00:17.820
rate limiting, request IDs, response
headers,

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:23.723
and proxy-aware behavior may all live
outside the individual handler.

6
00:00:23.803 --> 00:00:28.267
That can be powerful because it
centralizes controls,

7
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:35.043
but it also means the route-level security
story depends on layer order and scope.

8
00:00:35.123 --> 00:00:38.713
Middleware scope should be easy to
inspect.

9
00:00:38.793 --> 00:00:41.899
A global layer may affect every route,

10
00:00:41.979 --> 00:00:47.122
while a layer applied to a router group
affects only that group.

11
00:00:47.202 --> 00:00:51.666
If administrative routes, API routes,
health routes,

12
00:00:51.746 --> 00:00:55.822
and internal callbacks are composed
separately,

13
00:00:55.902 --> 00:01:01.045
reviewers should confirm which controls
apply to each branch.

14
00:01:01.125 --> 00:01:07.821
A route can look safe locally while
missing a required layer because of where it was

15
00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:08.678
mounted.

16
00:01:08.758 --> 00:01:14.969
CORS deserves careful review because it
defines browser trust boundaries.

17
00:01:15.049 --> 00:01:21.842
It should match real access requirements
rather than broad convenience defaults.

18
00:01:21.922 --> 00:01:28.424
A browser-facing service, a same-origin
application, an internal admin tool,

19
00:01:28.504 --> 00:01:33.356
and a machine-to-machine API may need
different behavior.

20
00:01:33.436 --> 00:01:38.676
CORS is not a replacement for
authentication or authorization,

21
00:01:38.756 --> 00:01:44.094
but misconfiguration can still expose
risky interaction paths.

22
00:01:44.174 --> 00:01:47.959
Security headers, request limits,
timeouts,

23
00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:54.152
and rate-limiting concepts should be
reviewed in the deployment context.

24
00:01:54.232 --> 00:01:57.726
Some headers may be set by the Axum
service,

25
00:01:57.806 --> 00:02:01.202
while others may be set by a reverse
proxy.

26
00:02:01.282 --> 00:02:04.485
Body limits may need to differ by route.

27
00:02:04.565 --> 00:02:09.029
Timeouts should reflect expected upstream
behavior.

28
00:02:09.109 --> 00:02:15.805
Forwarded headers should only be trusted
from known infrastructure boundaries.

29
00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:21.028
The best middleware design makes security
reasoning simpler.

30
00:02:21.108 --> 00:02:26.349
It should reduce repeated logic, make
route groups consistent,

31
00:02:26.429 --> 00:02:29.146
and expose assumptions clearly.

32
00:02:29.226 --> 00:02:34.661
If middleware hides too much behavior or
applies inconsistently,

33
00:02:34.741 --> 00:02:36.876
it can make review harder.

34
00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:40.837
The question is not only whether a layer
exists;

35
00:02:40.917 --> 00:02:46.740
it is whether the team can prove where it
applies and what it guarantees.
